DOCS_FRAGMENT("part:audit", "<p class=\"lede partlede\">Three dated code+logic reviews (2026-10-24, 2026-09-22, 2026-07-16): findings, fixes, verification. · source: <code>reference/AUDIT_2026-10-24.md</code></p><div class=\"sheetroot\" id=\"sheet-audit\"><nav class=\"wayfind\"><span class=\"wfl\">Record of change</span><a href=\"#roadmap\" onclick=\"show('roadmap')\">Roadmap <i>what is still open</i></a><a href=\"#whatsnew\" onclick=\"show('whatsnew')\">What&#x27;s new <i>what changed, and why it mattered</i></a><a href=\"#changelog\" onclick=\"show('changelog')\">Commits &amp; versions <i>the machine record</i></a><a href=\"#decisions\" onclick=\"show('decisions')\">Decisions <i>the register of choices</i></a><span class=\"here\">Audits <i>two dated reviews</i></span><a href=\"#validation\" onclick=\"show('validation')\">External validation <i>checked against Fidler</i></a></nav><div class=\"tldr\"><div class=\"tldr-h\">In short</div><p>The third audit, after <a href=\"#audit-first-audit-2026-07-16\">July's</a> and <a href=\"#audit-second-audit-2026-09-22\">September's</a>. Its\nscope is the 1910 place register: the <strong>frozen run</strong> (<code>notebooks/15_frozen_run.ipynb</code>, runner\n<code>utils/lexikon_1910/run_frozen.py</code>) and everything it calls, read the day after its first gold run\n(<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-118\">D-118</a>), plus the layout of the repository's code. Three independent read-only reviews ran on a\nfrozen copy of the code: a correctness review of the route, an audit of every register decision\nagainst what the route implements, and an audit of what the route delivers as place-level\nvariables. Every finding below was checked against the gold run's own output files, and every fix\nwas <strong>replayed offline</strong> on those files (nothing sent, no run folder written) before it was kept.\nDecisions: <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-119\">D-119</a> (code layout), <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-120\">D-120</a> (route fixes).</p></div><details class=\"feyn\"><summary>Explain it simply</summary><div class=\"feyn-b\"><p>Twice — once in July 2026 and again in September — the entire codebase was read line by line, deliberately looking for reasons to distrust it rather than reasons to ship it. This sheet is what the two passes found, newest first.</p><p>The September pass found its worst problem in the step that matches a legionnaire to the same man in the list of fallen Habsburg soldiers. Two records were called the same person when the names were similar enough — but the test scored the whole name at once, so a shared first name (there are a great many Františeks) could carry two different surnames over the line. A second rule trusted a note field that mostly says <i>which regiment keeps the death register</i>, not that the man served in the Legion. Once each part of the name had to match on its own and the note had to actually mention the Legion, the 2,239 matched men fell to 1,340. Fewer matches, but ones that are really the same person.</p><p>The July pass found the one below, and it is worth understanding even if you never touch the code.</p><p>It is the most dangerous shape a data bug takes. About 26,000 soldiers had a blank exit field — nothing was recorded about how their service ended. Somewhere in the chain, <i>blank</i> was converted to text, and the text of a missing value happens to begin with the letter N, which is also a valid exit code. So 26,000 blanks silently became 26,000 confident answers. Nothing crashed. Every total still added up. The true count was about 645.</p><p>That is why the checks that run on every build are mostly of the form <i>missing stays missing</i>. A bug that turns absence into a plausible value cannot be caught by looking at the output, because the output looks better than the truth.</p></div></details><nav class=\"toc\"><span>On this page</span><a href=\"#audit-a-the-code-layout-d-119\">A. The code layout (D-119)</a><a href=\"#audit-b-route-defects-found-fixed-measured\">B. Route defects — found, fixed, measured</a><a href=\"#audit-c-not-fixed-what-the-frozen-output-still-lacks-for-place-level-variables\">C. Not fixed — what the frozen output still lacks for place-level variables</a><a href=\"#audit-d-is-the-certification-procedure-enough\">D. Is the certification procedure enough?</a><a href=\"#audit-second-audit-2026-09-22\">Second audit — 2026-09-22</a><a href=\"#audit-first-audit-2026-07-16\">First audit — 2026-07-16</a></nav>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Status.</strong> Layout: done and verified (§A). Route defects: fixed, tested, replayed (§B). The\nGerman half of the gold re-runs on Heavyhorse6 with the fixes (sections 8–16, ≈ $0.5).\nNot fixed — proposals for the author (§C, §D). <strong>Done since (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-121\">D-121</a>):</strong> the readers' majority count (875 of 879\nCzech counts right), <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-58\">D-58</a>'s independence test in section 14, and a sealed German Böhmen certification\nhalf of four pages (0165, 0189, 0356, 0441).</p>\n</blockquote>\n<h2 id=\"audit-a-the-code-layout-d-119\">A. The code layout (D-119)</h2>\n<p>All code now lives under <code>utils/</code>; <code>reference/</code> holds data only.</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>What</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><code>utils/lexikon_1910/</code></td>\n<td>the register, all of it: the 14 library modules <code>lexikon_1910*.py</code> (from <code>utils/parsing/</code>) and the 31 scripts that drive them (from <code>reference/places/</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>utils/lexikon_1910/_archive/</code></td>\n<td>14 finished experiments and one-off measurements (bake-off, test arms, <code>measure_*</code>) and the failed glyph matcher <code>lexikon_1910_symbols.py</code> — kept runnable, not on the docs site</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>utils/reference_tools/&lt;topic&gt;/</code></td>\n<td>the 26 scripts that build and check the curated tables in <code>reference/&lt;topic&gt;/</code>: occupations, units, education, parties, places (district curation, amateur-theatre pilot), background (reading-pass tools)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>tests/_archive/</code></td>\n<td>the tests of two archived scripts</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Verified:</strong> 598 path references rewritten in 143 files, byte for byte (each file keeps its line\nendings; notebooks change only in their cell <em>sources</em>). 84 of 86 moved modules import as they\nactually run, each one's <code>ROOT</code> resolving to the repository (the other two are command-line tools\nthat need arguments, and compile). Every Python file parses. Every test passes (343 before the route\nfixes, 359 with the 16 they add). The docs site builds (39.3 MB; it now documents the register's\nmodules under a role of their own). Notebook 15 runs sections 1–7 with spending off. What still\nnames an old path: cell <em>outputs</em> of archived notebooks (historical printouts) and two audit\nscripts under <code>output/…/_archive/</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-b-route-defects-found-fixed-measured\">B. Route defects — found, fixed, measured</h2>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>Defect</th>\n<th>Effect on the gold</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>C1</td>\n<td>E3 sometimes files a reader's claim on the <em>neighbouring</em> glyph; Opus then rightly names that glyph and the box takes its value — the claim disappears and its neighbour is counted twice</td>\n<td>3 of the Czech 7 misses; 13 duplicated rows</td>\n<td>an answer that is the readers' <strong>majority</strong> reading at another mark of the <strong>same section</strong> and part rules on nothing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C2</td>\n<td>a mark's part was set from any E3 row, including Gemini's <code>unclaimed</code> rows, which only guess a part</td>\n<td>both Czech false claims, 2 misses</td>\n<td>the part comes from a reader's claim; an unclaimed row only where no reader claimed the box</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C3</td>\n<td>a token answer (\"is T printed here?\") was put on every box holding T; \"not printed\" then wiped <em>another</em> token a misplaced box also held</td>\n<td>German, with perfect answers: 11 true claims lost, 10 marks wrongly open</td>\n<td>the answer is a ruling on the <strong>claim</strong> (part, T), applied over every mark of the part (<code>claim_rulings</code>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>a mark Opus settled nothing on (\"none of these\", the outline on another mark) fell to the reader of record</td>\n<td>e.g. Gemini's <code>[mountain-inn]</code> against three <code>[tourist-hut]</code></td>\n<td>the readers' value stands if they all agree; otherwise the mark is <strong>open</strong> (the author's queue, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-111\">D-111</a>)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I1</td>\n<td>a call whose unit had no placed box was sent <strong>with no page image</strong> (the fallback searched the Czech gold folder only)</td>\n<td>German <code>0142-jicin-4-2</code>: \"No page image of the entry was provided\"</td>\n<td>the run's own sections first; a call with no image is not sent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I1′</td>\n<td>German bands were cut from boxes the gate had called untrusted</td>\n<td>(risk)</td>\n<td>token-only mode shows the whole section</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I2</td>\n<td>a claim flagged by the order rule alone was routed and never asked</td>\n<td>5 of the Czech gold's 11 order-only flags</td>\n<td>asked as a token question</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I3</td>\n<td>answers are keyed by call and question number: after a rebuild, old answers would be read against new questions</td>\n<td>(avoided once by moving a folder by hand)</td>\n<td>answers of calls whose questions changed are moved aside and the calls asked anew</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I4</td>\n<td>German headings print court names short (<code>Böhm. Aicha</code>); and the Czech register prints <em>Maršov</em> where the backbone has <em>Horní Maršov</em> — one court split, another never paired</td>\n<td>(outside the German gold pages)</td>\n<td>abbreviations written out; the two editions paired by the backbone's <code>court_id</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>I5</td>\n<td>the final inventory had no printed count (\"2 Sch\") and one row per <em>mark</em></td>\n<td>12.6 % of gold claims print a count</td>\n<td>one row per <strong>claim</strong>, with <code>count</code> and <code>count_source</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>certification pages could reach the author through the validation sample or the review queue, with the route's outlines, before their blind transcription</td>\n<td>(none read yet)</td>\n<td>no validation item and no review item from a sealed page; on a full run, no validation item from the in-sample gold pages</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><code>freeze()</code> silently rewrote a run's sheet and manifest — today's gold-run versions were overwritten by a laptop rebuild while this audit ran</td>\n<td>provenance only (Dropbox keeps the 16:10 versions)</td>\n<td>once reads exist the sheet is kept; a changed manifest is archived, with package versions and host</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>a batch-prepare cut short left a partial queue that was never completed</td>\n<td>(risk: crops read live at twice the price)</td>\n<td>queues are written as <code>.part</code> and named only when complete</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>calls were spent on the worked-example page, scored nowhere</td>\n<td>5 calls on the gold</td>\n<td>skipped on the Czech gold</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Replayed on the gold run's own files</strong> (Opus answers as returned on 2026-10-23):</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>reader of record alone</th>\n<th>route as run</th>\n<th>route fixed</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Czech gold, 881 claims</td>\n<td>F1 0.955 (31 false, 48 missed)</td>\n<td>0.995 (2 false, 7 missed)</td>\n<td><strong>0.999 (0 false, 2 missed)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>German gold, 343 claims, token questions answered by the gold (the ceiling)</td>\n<td>0.941</td>\n<td>0.899 (box questions)</td>\n<td><strong>0.975 (3 false, 14 missed)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Of the two Czech misses, one is open in the author's queue (5 open marks on the gold — ≈ 190 for\nthe Czech volume) and one is a mark no reader read and no detector boxed. Printed counts are right\non 866 of 879 true Czech claims. <strong>These gold figures are in-sample</strong> — the contrast sheet, the\nlocator, DINOv3's bank and the look-alike list were built on the same pages — so they show that the\nroute works, not its error rate; that comes only from the sealed pages (§D).</p>\n<p><strong>Measured and not adopted:</strong> sending DINOv3's <em>unclaimed</em> boxes (glyph-like boxes no reader\nclaimed — <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-95\">D-95</a>'s omission flags) to Opus. On the Czech gold there are 52 (15 in the offices\nsection); DINOv3 names a gold claim on 8 of them, and the route already holds all 8 from other\nmarks. It would add ≈ 50 questions per 13 pages and recover nothing.</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-c-not-fixed-what-the-frozen-output-still-lacks-for-place-level-variables\">C. Not fixed — what the frozen output still lacks for place-level variables</h2>\n<p>The route settles one thing: <strong>which pictogram institution each part has</strong>, now with its count.\nEverything else the register prints is read and stays in the passes' side files:</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Variable</th>\n<th>Read</th>\n<th>Settled into a final output</th>\n<th>Gold to score it</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>pictogram institutions (+ count)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td><strong>yes</strong></td>\n<td>yes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>institutions printed as <strong>abbreviations</strong> — district court, captaincy, tax office, notary, lawyers, deanery… (1,197 of Gemini's 1,213 decoded rows on the gold)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td><strong>no</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>school classes and <strong>language</strong>, cooperative kinds and language (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-70\">D-70</a>)</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>yes (264 rows)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the parts list — names, houses, population, part type</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no (type marks settled, then dropped)</td>\n<td>yes (660 rows)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the <strong>table</strong> — area, houses, population by sex, confession, language</td>\n<td>yes, twice</td>\n<td>no — the two reads are never reconciled</td>\n<td>yes (403 rows)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>postal district, station and walking hours, the legend</td>\n<td>yes</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>partly</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prague and Liberec (statutory towns: prose pages, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-82\">D-82</a>)</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>a <strong>place key</strong> — register municipality → <code>settlements_1910</code> / <code>okres_id</code></td>\n<td>no (the old anchors cover 104 of 199 blocks, on OCR names)</td>\n<td>no</td>\n<td>no</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Small additions, no new model calls (each ≈ ½–1½ days): settle the attributes by agreement across\nthe four passes; settle the parts list (and keep the type marks the ledger already settles);\n<strong>reconcile the two table reads</strong> cell by cell with the register's own arithmetic (I1–I5, I8) as\nthe tie-breaker; settle the abbreviation institutions the same way (they need a few gold pages\ncoded by the author first); put the court's <code>court_id</code> and <code>okres_id</code> on every final file; read\nthe Prague and Liberec prose pages (≈ 8 calls, &lt; $0.10). Bigger: the apparatus↔table key\n(municipality number within the court), anchoring each municipality to <code>settlements_1910</code> on the\nmodel-read names (scored on the gold pages), and a place table in <code>data/final</code> built only in 02.</p>\n<p>Decided but not implemented (from the decisions audit): the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-58\">D-58</a> independence test on the frozen\noutputs (the code exists in <code>score_r5_german_notes</code>); the one-shot certification scorer with\npage-clustered intervals and per-symbol rates (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-55\">D-55</a>, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-63\">D-63</a>); double-coding two pages (Stage 0); the\n<code>order_restarts</code> parsing queue (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-74\">D-74</a>); the one-request Gemini batch probe (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-117\">D-117</a>); prompt digests in\nthe manifest (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-109\">D-109</a>); running heads on scans 0077 and 0091 (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-87\">D-87</a>).</p>\n<h2 id=\"audit-d-is-the-certification-procedure-enough\">D. Is the certification procedure enough?</h2>\n<p><strong>Not yet — for the use it has to serve.</strong> It was designed to certify one number: how accurately\nthe route reads symbols, on eight sealed Czech pages (a systematic sample with a random start,\nseed 1910, scans 46 · 106 · 166 · 226 · 286 · 346 · 406 · 466; ≈ 560–580 claims). For that\nheadline it is sound: the pages are spread over the volume, they are sealed in code from every\nlearning component, and the route will read them with nothing tuned on them. What the thesis\nneeds is more than a headline: <strong>an error rate for each variable a regression will use, at the\nlevel the regression uses it, for every volume the data come from</strong> — and an estimator (DSL/PPI,\n<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-55\">D-55</a>) that needs a probability sample of units coded by hand. Against that, five gaps:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Rare institutions are not certifiable on eight pages.</strong> Common symbols (school, post, parish,\n   fire brigade) occur dozens of times a page; castles, poorhouses, ruins, railway stops,\n   pharmacies, savings banks and cooperatives occur a handful of times on eight pages — and they\n   are exactly where the route errs (the look-alike pairs). Eight pages are also only eight\n   clusters: with ≈ 70 claims a page and modest within-page correlation the effective sample is a\n   few hundred claims, so even the headline carries ± 1–2 points.</li>\n<li><strong>Omissions are invisible to the validation sample.</strong> It draws ledger <em>marks</em>; a symbol no\n   reader read and no detector boxed has no mark (the one Czech gold miss left is exactly that).\n   Recall needs whole entries transcribed blind.</li>\n<li><strong>The unit is wrong for the estimator.</strong> DSL/PPI correct a regression for prediction error\n   using a random sample of the regression's own units — municipalities or settlements — coded by\n   hand with known inclusion probabilities, <em>across the whole volume</em>. The sealed pages are eight\n   pages, and the validation sample is marks.</li>\n<li><strong>The German volumes have no certification at all.</strong> Böhmen has no <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-sealed-half-en\" data-gl=\"sealed half\">sealed half</a>, and its gold is\n   no longer a clean test (the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-118\">D-118</a>/<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-120\">D-120</a> fixes were chosen after reading it). Mähren and\n   Schlesien are planned to get one or two spot-check pages each — but for them the German\n   volume is the <em>only</em> source (the Czech edition covers Bohemia alone), so their variables would\n   rest on no measured error rate.</li>\n<li><strong>Only the symbols are covered.</strong> The table (population, houses, area), the attributes\n   (school language), the abbreviation institutions and the place linkage — all analysis\n   variables — have no certification design.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>An affordable design</strong> (the author's time is the cost; API cost is negligible):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pre-register before anything is opened</strong>: write the one-shot scorer and date the metrics in a\n  decision — claim-level P/R/F1 and per-symbol sensitivity/specificity with page-clustered\n  intervals, count accuracy, table cell accuracy, the acceptance bars, and the sample design below.</li>\n<li><strong>Keep the eight sealed pages</strong> as the headline, read once by the frozen run, scored once.</li>\n<li><strong>Add an entry-level probability sample across each volume</strong> — the DSL/PPI sample. Draw\n  municipalities (not pages), stratified by what the route did (agreed / Opus-settled / author)\n  and by whether the route predicts a rare symbol there, with known probabilities; the author\n  transcribes each <strong>whole entry</strong> blind (no outlines, no readings shown). It yields recall,\n  per-symbol rates for the rare symbols, the DSL weights, and — once the place key exists — the\n  linkage error on the same entries. Czech: start with ≈ 150 municipalities (≈ 3 minutes each,\n  ≈ 8 hours), extend if the intervals are too wide.</li>\n<li><strong>German</strong>: draw a sealed Böhmen certification set of 3–4 pages now, before anything else is\n  tuned on German pages; for Mähren and Schlesien, an entry-level sample of ≈ 60–80\n  municipalities each instead of 1–2 pages.</li>\n<li><strong>Table</strong>: certify on the sealed pages' table rows plus the register's own arithmetic on every\n  page — once the two reads are reconciled (§C).</li>\n<li><strong>Double-code two pages</strong> (one calibration, one fresh) to bound the transcriber's own error.</li>\n<li><strong>Back-check the high-value symbols against sources outside the register</strong> (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-104\">D-104</a>): railway\n  stations, post offices and parishes on a sample, against a railway gazetteer, the postal\n  directory and the 1910 diocesan schematism — the one check that can see an error the print and\n  its transcriber share.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>In total ≈ 25–35 hours of the author's time across the four volumes; it turns every place variable\ninto one with a measured error rate and makes the regressions on them correctable by design.</p><h2 id=\"audit-second-audit-2026-09-22\">Second audit — 2026-09-22</h2><p>The second full pass over the pipeline, two months after the <a href=\"#audit-first-audit-2026-07-16\">first</a>. Its\nscope is <strong>data manipulation and cleaning</strong>: every module notebook 02 calls\n(<code>utils/parsing/*</code>), <code>utils/linkage.py</code>, the QA gate and the validation workbook\n(<code>utils/validation/*</code>), <code>utils/tables</code>, notebooks 02 and 03, and the curated inputs they read\n(district and Slovak curation, the Prague-suburb table, the occupation curation and HISCO\nauthority, the party register). <strong>Out of scope by instruction:</strong> retrieval — the <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"csol\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">csol.cz</a> and\n<a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> scrapers, the lexicon and Kramerius downloads, the <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> <em>fetch</em> — and the 1910-register\nOCR sub-project (<code>lexikon_1910_*</code>, notebooks 05/06), which does not feed <code>data/final</code>.</p>\n<p>Every module was read line by line and every finding below was <strong>measured on the data on\ndisk</strong> (the 2026-09-03 build), not inferred from the code. Severity: <strong>[A] changes an analysis\nvariable</strong> · <strong>[B] logic or robustness, narrower impact</strong> · <strong>[C] documentation drift</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<h3 id=\"fix-status-all-a-b-and-c-items-fixed-in-code-notebooks-02-and-03-re-run\">✅ Fix status: all A, B and C items fixed in code, <strong>notebooks 02 and 03 re-run</strong></h3>\n<p>The fixes are in the modules listed against each finding; the QA gate\n(<code>utils/validation/data_checks.py</code> 1.12.0) gained a hard invariant for every defect class,\nand on the old build those invariants fail exactly where this document says they should\n(81/97 passing, all 16 failures below). Measured before/after numbers are in §E.\nDecisions taken on the way are <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-59\">D-59</a> to <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-62\">D-62</a>.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p><strong>The headline.</strong> The gate passed 72/72 on the old build, and the old build was wrong in the\nplace the thesis leans on hardest: <strong>the cross-dataset linkage</strong>. Its name test let a shared\ngiven name carry two different surnames, and its second criterion rested on a note field that\nmostly records <em>which interwar regiment keeps the death register</em>, not Legion service. Of\n2,239 \"confirmed\" overlaps, a large share were coincidences of name, year and district. The\nrest of the findings are smaller and each is one of the bug shapes this repository already\nknows; two are new (§F).</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"a-defects-that-change-an-analysis-variable\">A. Defects that change an analysis variable</h3>\n<h4 id=\"a1-the-linkage-name-test-was-carried-by-the-given-name-linkagefind_overlap\">A1. The linkage name test was carried by the given name — <code>linkage.find_overlap</code></h4>\n<p>One <code>token_sort_ratio</code> over the concatenated \"surname given\" string had to reach 80. A long\ncommon given name dominates that ratio: <code>STANĚK František</code> ↔ <code>Soja František</code> scored exactly\n80, as did <code>ŠVEC František</code> ↔ <code>Sládek František</code>. Measured on <code>overlap_matches.csv</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">DOB-confirmed (high)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">note-confirmed (medium)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>pairs</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1,776</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">463</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>identical surnames</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">1,121</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">218</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>surname similarity &lt; 60 after Czech/German spelling folds</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>263</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>126</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(BERAN ↔ Bureš, ČERNÝ ↔ Cvrk, MORAVEC ↔ Málek.) And the matcher was not one-to-one:\n<strong>111 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> records were claimed by two to four legionnaires</strong> (231 rows, so at least 120\nwrong by construction) — one Zdice-born František by Šůra, Škvára, Schoř <em>and</em> Šinágl —\nwhile the <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>-side flag kept only one, so the two files disagreed on 120 links.</p>\n<p>Why it matters: an exact-DOB collision is rare <em>for one person</em>, not across 186k × 133k\nrecords. Over every exact-DOB, same-given-name pair in the two files, pairs whose surnames\nstart with <strong>different</strong> letters — chance by construction — still reach the 80–90 similarity\nband.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>linkage</code> 1.3.0, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-59\">D-59</a>).</strong> Surname and given name scored separately on a phonetic key\nthat folds the orthography the archives mix (<code>sch</code>→s, <code>tsch</code>/<code>cz</code>/<code>tz</code>/<code>z</code>→c, <code>w</code>→v,\n<code>y</code>/<code>ie</code>→i, umlaut digraphs, doubled letters); surname ≥ 90, or ≥ 85 when the birth okres\nagrees too; given name ≥ 80. Blocking on the <em>phonetic</em> initial (so Wesely meets Veselý).\nOne-to-one on both sides, with an exact tie <strong>refused</strong>, never broken by order. A pair whose\nfull dates of birth both exist and disagree is refused.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a2-note_legion-read-archival-provenance-as-legion-service-habsburg_legion_re\">A2. <code>note_legion</code> read archival provenance as Legion service — <code>habsburg._LEGION_RE</code></h4>\n<p>Any \"čs. … <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-pluk-cs\" data-gl=\"pluk\">pluk</a>/<a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-prapor-cs\" data-gl=\"prapor\">prapor</a> N\" in the <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> note counted as a Legion cross-link. That phrase is\noverwhelmingly <strong>provenance</strong>: the interwar Czechoslovak army's regiments kept the death\nregisters of their recruiting district, so a note reads <em>\"náhradní prapor čs. pěšího pluku\n33\"</em> or <em>\"Opsáno na podkladě: úmrtní knihy, … čs. pěšího pluku 33\"</em> for a man who fell in\nSerbia in September 1914.</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>note_legion (old)</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">records</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>\"čs. … pluk/prapor\" register phrases</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">10,509</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Seznam vozatajského pluku 8\" (a k.u.k. train-regiment list)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">145</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\"Sibiř\" (mostly POWs)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">4</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>explicit Legion vocabulary (legionář, <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-legie-cs\" data-gl=\"legie\">legie</a>, Kancelář legií)</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">≈384</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>total</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>11,042</strong></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>The flagged men died at a <strong>median of 1915</strong>; 1,246 died in Serbia <em>in 1914</em>, before any\nCzechoslovak unit existed. A placebo settles it: <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> records with a provenance note match a\nlegionnaire on name + birth year + okres at <strong>2.9 %</strong>, <em>below</em> the <strong>4.8 %</strong> of records with no\nnote at all; genuine Legion notes match at 59.7 %. Meanwhile <strong>372 <code>KLEG</code></strong> notes (the\nabbreviation of <em>Kancelář legií</em>) were <strong>missed</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>habsburg</code> 1.2.0).</strong> <code>note_legion</code> = explicit Legion vocabulary only (legionář, legie,\nKLEG, Kancelář legií, čs. dobrovolník, rota Nazdar, <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-česká-družina-cs\" data-gl=\"česká družina\">Česká družina</a>, the twelve Russian\nrifle regiments \"čs. <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-střelecký-pluk-cs\" data-gl=\"střelecký pluk\">střelecký pluk</a> 1–12\", never with a register page reference). The\nregister unit moves to a new provenance column, <code>note_register_ref</code>, so nothing is lost.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a3-criterion-b-note-confirmed-was-mostly-name-year-okres\">A3. Criterion B (\"note-confirmed\") was mostly name + year + okres</h4>\n<p>Of the 463 note-confirmed pairs: <strong>195</strong> rested on a provenance note, <strong>182</strong> on another\n\"čs. pluk … str.\" register note and <strong>86</strong> on genuine Legion vocabulary. The provenance group\nlooks nothing like a real overlap — 5.6 % died within a month of the legionnaire's capture,\n6.2 % after his enrolment, 107 are killed-in-action (65 of them <em>before</em> the legionnaire was\neven captured) — and <strong>31</strong> pairs carried two full dates of birth that disagree. Criterion B\ntherefore accepted, for 9.5k noted records, the very evidence <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-01\">D-01</a> refuses as too weak for\nthe other 126k (6,057 such coincidences dropped). Fixed by A1 + A2.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a4-214-galicia-born-legionnaires-in-the-slovak-jaras-halic-records_geo_huadd_jaras_id\">A4. 214 Galicia-born legionnaires in the Slovak járás Halič — <code>records_geo_hu.add_jaras_id</code></h4>\n<p>\"Halič\" is <strong>Galicia</strong> in Czech and a small Novohrad town (Gács) in Slovak. Resolved by name,\n214 legionnaires born in Lvov, Přemyšl, Kraków, Kolomyja, Tarnopol… were placed in járás Halič\n(131 by the exact string, 83 by fuzzy strings like \"Brody - Halič\") and <strong>carried its census\ncovariates</strong> in <code>legionnaires_census_hu_1910</code> — although their own <code>birth_land</code> already said\nGalicia. 110 more on the home role. Of 218 records in the unit, 4 were Slovak.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>records_geo_hu</code> 1.4.0).</strong> <code>_AMBIGUOUS_SEATS = {\"halic\"}</code> is never resolved from the\ndistrict string; genuine Slovak Halič births still resolve through the corroborated\nsettlement pass.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a5-theatre-imputation-sent-us-applicants-to-britain-and-poles-to-the-balkans-theater\">A5. Theatre imputation sent US applicants to Britain and Poles to the Balkans — <code>theater</code></h4>\n<p>Pass 3 mapped US application cities to <strong>Britain/Dominions</strong>; in the data, US applicants whose\nown unit names a theatre served in <strong>France</strong> 1,659 times and in a British or Dominion unit 26\ntimes (98.4 %). 248 unit-less US applicants were imputed against that. And <code>\"Polská\"</code> sent all\n<strong>41</strong> Polish-Legion records to Serbia/Balkans, though their units are Haller's <em>Armée\npolonaise</em> (France), <em>Dutova armáda</em> and <em>Krymský pluk</em> (Russia). Fidler's check (2026-07-20)\ncovered CS-Legion records only, so neither was ever tested.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>theater</code> 1.1.0, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-60\">D-60</a>).</strong> US cities → France; the Polish rule removed; <em>Armée\npolonaise</em> → France; a self-test.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a6-datafinal-predated-the-code-and-the-gate-could-not-see-it\">A6. <code>data/final</code> predated the code, and the gate could not see it</h4>\n<p>Education 2.1.0 and affiliations 2.1.0 (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-51\">D-51</a>, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-52\">D-52</a>) landed on 2026-09-07; <code>data/final</code> was\nwritten on 2026-09-03. <code>pol_recorded</code>, <code>education_semesters</code>, <code>education_top_imputed</code> did not\nexist, the <code>pol_*</code> dummies were still False on 93k blank cells, and the eight checks written\nfor those releases were <strong>guarded on the missing columns and silently skipped</strong>. CLAUDE.md\nsaid the build was current.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>data_checks</code> 1.12.0).</strong> A hard check that <code>data/final</code> carries every column the\ncurrent schema emits; it fails on any stale build. Notebooks re-run.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a7-birthhome_cisleithanian-contradicted-the-corrected-land-locations-records_geo\">A7. <code>birth/home_cisleithanian</code> contradicted the corrected land — <code>locations</code>, <code>records_geo</code></h4>\n<p>The flag came from <code>locations.py</code>'s text-only land and was never re-derived after the land\nwas corrected: <strong>2,270</strong> Czech-lands <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> births were flagged <em>not</em> Cisleithanian — 1,747 of\nthem in okres Uherské Hradiště or Uherský Brod, because the bare substring <code>uhersk</code> meant\nHungary — and <strong>8,997</strong> Slovak births were flagged Cisleithanian.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix.</strong> <code>records_geo.sync_cisleithanian</code> derives the flag from the corrected land after every\nland update; <code>locations</code> 1.1.0 matches country nouns as words.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a8-exit_date-was-never-range-checked-exit_codesadd_exit_columns\">A8. <code>exit_date</code> was never range-checked — <code>exit_codes.add_exit_columns</code></h4>\n<p>Every other date is clipped; the exit date — the hazard model's event time — ran from\n<strong>1818 to 2192</strong>: 66 before 1914 (1818–1825 century slips), 149 after 1923 (<code>28.6.19520</code> read\nas 1952), 308 exits before enrolment (34 deserters). And <strong>3,930</strong> exit strings that state a\nyear (<code>1920 ( Vrátil se do vlasti )</code>) got no date at all (69 deserters).</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (<code>exit_codes</code> 1.2.0 + <code>dates</code> 1.2.0, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-61\">D-61</a>).</strong> The exit date is parsed from the text\n<em>before</em> the parenthesis (so the \"1918\" in <em>Sloužil v čs. armádě po r. 1918</em> can never be read)\nthrough the shared date machinery: range 1914–1939, the century-slip fix both ways, month-year\nand year fallbacks, and a new <code>exit_date_precision</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"b-logic-and-robustness\">B. Logic and robustness</h3>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>#</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>Defect (measured)</th>\n<th>Fix</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>B1</td>\n<td><code>units.extract_army</code></td>\n<td>Army read from the <strong>first</strong> parenthetical: 14 \"Royal Fusiliers (R.F.)\" → French Army (<code>r.f.</code>), ~150 British records got a regimental nickname as their army</td>\n<td>last parenthetical, trailing <code>?</code> stripped (<code>units</code> 1.3.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B2</td>\n<td><code>affiliations</code></td>\n<td>Bare <code>socialista</code> coded soc_dem while bare <code>demokrat</code> is refused — though Č24 <em>Česká strana socialistická</em> is the national socialists' own name; <code>národní socialista</code> became a social democrat; tokens unanchored (<code>karpatorus</code> → Russian)</td>\n<td><code>socialista</code> refused; national-socialist forms → nar_soc; word-start anchoring (<code>affiliations</code> 2.2.0); 18 records change</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B3</td>\n<td><code>occupations</code> <code>ABBREV[\"zem\"]</code></td>\n<td><code>zem.dělník</code> (18) expanded to <em>zemský</em> → 99900 / HISCLASS 13 instead of <em>zemědělský</em> 62105 / 12; \"rolník, zem. dělník\" lost the farmer</td>\n<td>context expansion before <code>dělník</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B4</td>\n<td><code>occupations</code> compound rules</td>\n<td>454 comma lists (464 records) coded on the <strong>last</strong> segment's head noun, the first occupation filed as a \"qualifier\" (\"rolník, tovární dělník\" → 99900, qualifier <em>rolník tovární</em>)</td>\n<td>lists coded segment-wise as <code>dual</code>, first occupation primary (<code>occupations</code> 1.4.0)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B5</td>\n<td><code>habsburg.RANK_TERMS</code></td>\n<td>Unanchored: 428 notes with <em>dělostřelec</em> returned <em>střelec</em>, 5 <em>generálmajor</em> returned <em>major</em></td>\n<td>word-start anchored, <code>generálmajor</code> listed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B6</td>\n<td><code>records_geo</code>, <code>records_geo_hu</code></td>\n<td>Land never refreshed after the Slovak passes (1,041 legion / 2,707 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> járás-placed births with land NA); substring markers mislabelled <em>Polská Ostrava</em> → Poland, <em>Nemecká Ľupča</em> → Germany, <em>Kapušany</em> → Overseas (\"usa\"), <em>Ruskov</em>, <em>Prusko</em> → Russia</td>\n<td><code>set_land_from_jaras</code>; whole-word country nouns</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B7</td>\n<td><code>records_geo.resolve_prague_suburbs</code></td>\n<td>1,537 births (1,468 csol + 69 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>; 1,682 more on the home role) moved to their 1910 suburb okres lost their standardised municipality (attributes keyed on the pre-move okres)</td>\n<td>attributes re-keyed after the move</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B8</td>\n<td><code>wikidata.add_settlement_coords</code></td>\n<td>95 settlements in different 1910 okresy shared 46 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> points (BĚLÁ Semily / Turnov) — one of the two places got the other's coordinates</td>\n<td>a point claimed from &gt; 1 1910 okres is refused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B9</td>\n<td>QA gate</td>\n<td>Coordinate-accuracy check counted rows the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-35\">D-35</a> repair snapped into agreement (95.9 %)</td>\n<td>snapped rows counted as the disagreements they were (90.0 %)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B10</td>\n<td>notebook 03</td>\n<td>\"Bolshevik Coup\" drawn at <strong>1917-09-07</strong> on three figures</td>\n<td>1917-11-07 (25 Oct Julian)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B11</td>\n<td>notebook 03</td>\n<td>The occupation-qualifier table's \"(bare)\" rows were 11–91 % dual records (zřízenec 111/122)</td>\n<td>families by whole-token head, duals excluded</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B12</td>\n<td>notebook 03</td>\n<td>Rank-reset histogram and table on the full column, not the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-04\">D-04</a> combat-rank headline</td>\n<td>headline on <code>legion_noncombat == False</code>, full column as robustness row</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B13</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Probable <strong>duplicate persons</strong> inside each file — same surname, given name, full DOB and birth municipality (e.g. ADÁMEK Karel 11917 / 130959): 625 csol and 281 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> rows</td>\n<td>flagged in new <code>dup_group</code>; nothing dropped</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B14</td>\n<td><code>dates</code></td>\n<td>Legion <code>death_date</code> clipped to 1914–1923 drops 8,034 of 12,480 recorded deaths (veterans); the docstring said \"a few\"; raw string not kept</td>\n<td>docstring corrected; <code>death_date_raw</code> kept</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B15</td>\n<td><code>dates</code></td>\n<td>2,440 capture and 2,463 enrolment strings with only a month or a year (<code>4.1918</code>, <code>1918</code>, impossible <code>29.2.1918</code>) dropped without a flag</td>\n<td>month/year fallbacks + <code>*_date_precision</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B16</td>\n<td><code>build_validation_workbook</code></td>\n<td>Re-ran the Slovak passes without <code>resolve_jaras_twins</code> (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-19\">D-19</a>)</td>\n<td>twin resolution + land refresh added</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B17</td>\n<td>several</td>\n<td>Missing coded as False (the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-52\">D-52</a> shape): <code>cause_*</code> on 8,297 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> records with a blank cause, <code>in_captivity</code>, <code>*_rank_jd</code> on 34,337 blank rank cells, <code>legion_noncombat</code> on 19,270</td>\n<td><code>&lt;NA&gt;</code> on blank source</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B18</td>\n<td><code>ranks.RANK_COMMON</code></td>\n<td><code>podkapitán</code> had an ordinal but no common grade (4 records, 1 reset lost)</td>\n<td>common grade 12</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B19</td>\n<td>data dictionary</td>\n<td>9 columns per file undocumented (linkage flags, <code>*_court_snapped</code>, <code>*_obec_sk</code>, <code>*_okres_prague</code>); <code>exit_*</code> had no descriptions</td>\n<td>documented; hard check</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B20</td>\n<td>census codebook</td>\n<td>12 justice/personnel columns renamed after the codebook was built were missing; three pairs (deposits vs withdrawals ×2, passbooks at start vs end of year) differed only by <code>_2</code>; <code>lit_illiterate</code> described as \"read write\"</td>\n<td>curated names; codebook rows added; hard check that every panel column is documented</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B21</td>\n<td><code>units</code></td>\n<td><code>regiment_plot</code> always \"Other (Unknown)\" (theatre added later); <code>unit_report</code> and the nb03 size scatter on <code>regiment</code>, not <code>unit_key</code></td>\n<td>reports and scatter on <code>unit_key</code></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B22</td>\n<td><code>ranks._resolve</code></td>\n<td>Tied top Legion ordinal (praporčík / kadet / důstojnický zástupce at 7) resolved by word order into different common grades — latent, 0 cases</td>\n<td>ties broken by the higher common grade</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B23</td>\n<td><code>lexikon</code> (D1 residual)</td>\n<td><strong>116 register entries</strong> whose headword carries a mixed-case parenthetical, a <code>t.</code> alternate or a spaced separator never started an entry; they vanished and their text polluted the previous settlement's okres timeline</td>\n<td>headword pattern widened; alternates kept as aliases</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B24</td>\n<td><code>records_geo</code> okres lookup</td>\n<td>PDF-broken spaces (<code>Prachat ice</code>): 81 settlements unmapped, 169 more only via fuzzy</td>\n<td>space-free exact lookup, collisions refused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B25</td>\n<td><code>districts</code></td>\n<td>Six <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"mpidr_2012\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">MPIDR</a> German names mangled (<code>Sziszkow</code> = Žižkow, <code>Rosznau</code>, <code>Kneszna</code>…) in delivered <code>okres_de</code>/<code>court_de</code></td>\n<td><code>GERMAN_NAME_FIX</code></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"c-documentation-drift-fixed-in-the-same-pass\">C. Documentation drift (fixed in the same pass)</h3>\n<p>CLAUDE.md called <code>data/final</code> current and quoted a 66/66 gate; <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-01\">D-01</a>, PIPELINE, VARIABLES and\nthe linkage docstring called the linkage \"near-certain\"; notebook 02 cited <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-24\">D-24</a> for the HISCAM\ndrop (it is <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-30\">D-30</a>); DECISIONS listed <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-49\">D-49</a> after <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-50\">D-50</a>; <code>spatial.py</code> still quoted \"7,248 … 68 % …\nstructural\" (the 68 % was a coordinate bug, D2 of the first audit); <code>districts.py</code> described a\ngazetteer tier retired in July; <code>schema.py</code> named notebooks that no longer exist, offered a\n<code>low</code> linkage tier never emitted, omitted the <code>continuation</code> track and described a\n<code>education_complete</code> branch removed in 2.1.0; the education TRACKS comment contradicted 2.1.0;\noccupations quoted 3.1 / 4.8 % against 3.38 / 6.06 % elsewhere; affiliations said 93,319 blank\ncells, not 93,377; <code>districts_hu</code> said 184 units, not 192; and the two datasets' <code>theater</code>\ncolumns share a name but not a label set (only Russia, Italy and France compare) — now said\nwhere the column is described.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"d-checked-and-found-sound\">D. Checked and found sound</h3>\n<p><code>clean</code>; the exit-code token parser after the July fix; <code>provenance</code>; <code>religion</code>; census\npooling (the justice columns are three-year means, so summing is valid); <code>census_hu</code>;\n<code>districts_hu</code>; <code>lexikon_sk</code> (no seat-key collisions, every row reconciles); <code>spatial</code>\nprojection, parity and snap logic; frame exclusivity; the twin and Prague label guards; rank\nself-resolution; theatre pass 2 (175 rows, no ties); occupation fold collisions (8, all the\nsame code); the curated inputs (district and Slovak curation, Prague suburbs, occupation\ncuration and HISCO authority, party register 12/12); <code>latex_tables</code>.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"e-post-fix-verification-measured-on-the-re-run-datafinal\">E. Post-fix verification — measured on the re-run <code>data/final</code></h3>\n<p>Notebook 02 re-executed top to bottom (nbconvert, exit 0) and then notebook 03 (exit 0; 28\nfigures and the LaTeX tables regenerated). The QA gate passes <strong>107 / 107</strong> on the new build;\non the old build the same gate passes 81 / 97, every failure one of the findings above. Every\nfigure below is read off the rebuilt files and compared with a copy of the 2026-09-03 build.</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>check</th>\n<th>2026-09-03 build</th>\n<th>re-run 2026-09-23</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>rows legion / habsburg (no join blow-up)</td>\n<td>132,993 / 186,192</td>\n<td><strong>132,993 / 186,192</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>confirmed overlaps (DOB + note)</td>\n<td>2,239 = 1,776 + 463</td>\n<td><strong>1,340 = 1,201 + 139</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> ids linked more than once</td>\n<td>120</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>linked pairs with disagreeing full DOBs</td>\n<td>31</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>old pairs kept</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>1,176 of 2,239; 164 new — 86 note-confirmed (74 on a <code>KLEG</code> note the old pattern missed) and 78 DOB-confirmed, mostly men whose csol name carries a second spelling or a Russian baptismal name (<code>DRAXL DRAKSL</code>, <code>Josef Sergej</code>) that the old whole-string score penalised</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>note_legion</code> (<a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>)</td>\n<td>11,042</td>\n<td><strong>966</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>note_register_ref</code> (provenance, new)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>10,548</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>legion births in járás Halič (of which Galicia-born)</td>\n<td>218 (214)</td>\n<td><strong>7 (0)</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>járás-placed births whose land is not Slovakia (legion / <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>)</td>\n<td>1,278 / 2,724</td>\n<td><strong>0 / 0</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> Czech-lands births flagged not Cisleithanian</td>\n<td>2,270</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> Slovak births flagged Cisleithanian</td>\n<td>8,997</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>US applicants in France / Britain-Dominions</td>\n<td>1,659 / 274</td>\n<td><strong>1,911 / 26</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Polish-Legion records in Serbia/Balkans</td>\n<td>41 of 41</td>\n<td><strong>1</strong> (France 21, Russia 12, Unknown 6, Italy 1)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>legion_army</code> British / French Army</td>\n<td>162 / 175</td>\n<td><strong>306 / 161</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>deserted</code></td>\n<td>3,717</td>\n<td>3,717 (unchanged)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>exit_date</code> non-null (range)</td>\n<td>101,700 (1818 – 2192)</td>\n<td><strong>105,527 (1914 – 1939)</strong>: 101,662 day, 461 month, 3,404 year</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>deserters with an exit date</td>\n<td>3,631</td>\n<td><strong>3,697</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>capture_date</code> / <code>enroll_date</code></td>\n<td>98,995 / 118,842</td>\n<td><strong>100,436 / 120,868</strong> (1,415 / 1,999 month- or year-precise)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pol_recorded</code> (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-52\">D-52</a>, now in the data)</td>\n<td>absent</td>\n<td>39,616</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pol_soc_dem</code> / <code>pol_nar_soc</code></td>\n<td>17,334 / 6,536</td>\n<td>17,317 / 6,541</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>education_semesters</code> / <code>education_top_imputed</code> (<a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-51\">D-51</a>, now in the data)</td>\n<td>absent</td>\n<td>722 / 1,112</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>records whose HISCLASS changed (B3, B4)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>403</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>occupation_match</code> dual / head / prefix</td>\n<td>6,518 / 4,252 / 2,091</td>\n<td>7,039 / 3,790 / 2,018</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>rank_reset</code>, combat ranks (the <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-04\">D-04</a> headline)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>N 85,698 · demoted 17.9 % · unchanged 78.0 % · promoted 4.2 % · mean −0.36</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>legion_noncombat</code> / <code>habsburg_rank_jd</code> / <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> <code>cause_KIA</code> → <code>&lt;NA&gt;</code> on blank source</td>\n<td>0 / 0 / 0</td>\n<td>19,270 / 34,337 / 8,297</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prague-suburb birth moves with a standardised municipality (legion / <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>)</td>\n<td>0 of 1,468 / 0 of 69</td>\n<td><strong>1,364 of 1,468 / 69 of 69</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>probable duplicate-person rows (<code>dup_group</code>, legion / <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>625 / 281</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>settlements / with 1910 okres / geocoded</td>\n<td>18,666 / 16,538 / 5,912</td>\n<td><strong>18,916 / 16,755</strong> / 5,841</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>settlement timelines carrying another entry's text</td>\n<td>116</td>\n<td><strong>4</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> points shared by settlements in different 1910 okresy</td>\n<td>46 (95 settlements)</td>\n<td><strong>0</strong> (5 points remain shared inside one okres)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>birth coordinates (legion / <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a>)</td>\n<td>58,314 / 68,616</td>\n<td>58,074 / 68,450 (legion: 516 lost, 386 of them on a refused shared point; 276 gained from recovered settlements)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>settlement coordinates in their own okres, before snapping</td>\n<td>(gate reported 95.9 % incl. snapped rows)</td>\n<td><strong>90.0 %</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>data-dictionary rows</td>\n<td>314</td>\n<td>341 (every column documented)</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What moved in the results, and what did not. <strong>Desertion itself did not move</strong> (3,717). The\ntheatre shares barely did (France 7.8 → 8.0 %, Britain/Dominions 0.5 → 0.3 %). The <strong>linkage\nlost 40 % of its pairs</strong>, and that is the change a reader of the comparison design must know\nabout: the overlap used as a control is 1,340 men, not 2,239, and the pairs that left were\ndisproportionately the note-confirmed ones (463 → 139). The combat-rank reset reads 17.9 %\ndemoted against 18.9 % on the inclusive column.</p>\n<h4 id=\"re-run-protocol\">Re-run protocol</h4>\n<p><code>notebooks/02_data_cleaning.ipynb</code> → <code>notebooks/03_visualisation.ipynb</code>, then\n<code>DOCS_NOINDEX=1 python utils/docs/build_docs.py</code>. The gate's first check now fails on any\n<code>data/final</code> older than the code, so a stale build can no longer pass silently.</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"f-two-new-bug-shapes\">F. Two new bug shapes</h3>\n<p>The six shapes in the first audit's lessons all recur here — an unguarded name match (A4),\nmissing values coded as content (B17), unanchored substrings (A7, B2, B5, B6), rules narrower\nthan their source (B23, B1), order deciding a tie (A1, B22), a permissive rule winning the race\n(B4). Two are new:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A similarity score dominated by its uninformative part.</strong> Concatenating surname and given\n   name let the part everyone shares (František) carry the part that identifies (the surname).\n   <em>Rule:</em> score each field on its own and require each to pass; check the threshold against\n   pairs that are wrong by construction (here: different initials).</li>\n<li><strong>A flag read off a field that records something else.</strong> The <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> note mostly records\n   <em>where the register is kept</em>; reading it as <em>what the man did</em> produced 10k false flags that\n   then fed a \"gold standard\". <em>Rule:</em> before a free-text field becomes evidence, tabulate the\n   flag against a variable that cannot be true for it (death in 1914 vs. a unit founded in\n   1917).</li>\n</ol><h2 id=\"audit-first-audit-2026-07-16\">First audit — 2026-07-16</h2><p>Full review of the pipeline (all <code>utils/</code> modules, the three stage notebooks, the\nreference docs) for code errors, logic/theory errors, and doc–code drift. Every\nfinding below was verified against the actual code and, where it matters, against\nthe raw/final data on disk. Companion docs written in the same pass:\n<a href=\"#pipeline\"><code>PIPELINE.md</code></a> (what each script does) and <a href=\"#variables\"><code>VARIABLES.md</code></a>\n(every constructed variable, with build formulas).</p>\n<p>Severity: <strong>[A] fix before analysis</strong> · <strong>[B] fix when touching the module</strong> ·\n<strong>[C] cosmetic / documentation</strong>.</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><strong>Sequel (2026-07-20).</strong> This audit was <em>internal</em> — it read the code against itself and\nagainst the data. An <em>external</em> pass followed once Fidler's (2025) encyclopedia became\navailable: <a href=\"#validation\"><code>VALIDATION_FIDLER.md</code></a>. It confirmed the repairs below\n(notably that the <code>exit_N</code> fix leaves the Russia exit codes reconciling to within 5 % of\nthe encyclopedia's repatriation totals) and found two defects this pass had missed —\nLegion generals scored as field officers, and <code>regiment</code> conflating armies. Both are now\nfixed and guarded. The lesson is the obvious one: checking a source against itself cannot\nfind a gap that is <em>absent</em> from it, such as a rank the ladder never contained.</p>\n<h3 id=\"fix-status-applied-and-notebook-02-re-run-2026-07-16-datafinal-is-current\">✅ Fix status: APPLIED <strong>and notebook 02 re-run</strong> (2026-07-16) — <code>data/final</code> is current</h3>\n<p>All A, B and C items are <strong>fixed in source</strong> except the two the author deferred:\n<strong>B2</strong> (nadlékař stays deliberately unscored — documented in <code>rank_ladders.md</code>\n§A1b instead) and <strong>B3</strong> (education classification to be revisited later — code\nuntouched). <strong>B3 was closed on 2026-09-02</strong>, once two sources arrived that could\nsettle it: <code>education.py</code> <strong>2.0.0</strong> rebuilds the variable on the 1869 ladder, with\n<code>pok.</code> off the ordinal entirely (8,117 records leave the Gymnasium bucket) and the\ndot-less tokens this section listed now parsing — coverage 96.7 % → <strong>99.1 %</strong>. The\nsection's own suggestion, a <em>Vocational continuation</em> level, was <strong>not</strong> taken: a\ncontinuation school was compulsory schooling and selected on nothing, so it is a\nflag, not a rung. See <a href=\"#education\">Education ladder</a> and <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-08\">D-08</a>, which stays open on\nthe Hungarian frame alone. The verification pass surfaced one <strong>major upgrade to A3</strong>: the old\nparser fabricated exit code <strong>N on all 26,386 empty cells</strong> (<code>pd.NA</code> → the string\n<code>\"&lt;NA&gt;\"</code> → letter N) — the final data's 27,034 \"Missing\" are ~97% fabricated,\ntrue count 644 (details + full delta table in A3). It also corrected a stale doc\nnumber: spatial-court vs name-okres agreement is 68%, not 91% (§E).\nBeyond the items below, the fix round also added, prompted by the author's\n\"Praha in Czechia vs. Slovakia\" concern:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Praha added to the Czech settlement crosswalk</strong> (curated row in\n  <code>lexikon.py</code> — the register has no PRAHA headword, leaving ~1.8k bare-\"Praha\"\n  <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> births with no Czech candidate at all);</li>\n<li><strong>cross-frame homonym guard</strong> on BOTH bare-name tiers (a municipality name\n  present in both countries' settlement frames is refused without a positive\n  signal — <code>records_geo.settlement_name_set</code> + <code>exclude_names</code>, wired in\n  notebook 02 §4);</li>\n<li><strong>corroborate-or-refuse</strong> in the SK settlement pass (a district string that\n  contradicts every candidate obec now refuses the match instead of falling\n  back — kills Volhynian <em>Dubno</em> → SK) + a positively-foreign land veto;</li>\n<li>a <strong>frame-exclusivity assertion</strong> in notebook 02 §4 (okres_id XOR jaras_id);</li>\n<li><code>_norm</code> now expands interior \"n.\" → \"nad\" (recovers <em>Ústí n. Labem</em>, 50 rec.);</li>\n<li>curation fix: Neubenatek <code>cs</code> = <em>Nové Benátky</em> (1910), <code>cs_modern</code> = <em>Benátky\n  nad Jizerou</em> (46 rec.); alias <em>Česká Třebová</em> → okres Lanškroun (21 rec.);</li>\n<li>Habsburg <code>podpraporčík</code> (n≈9) dropped like podporučík (B1 resolved as drop);</li>\n<li>affiliations: <code>rusín</code> (n=9) now its own <code>pol_rusyn</code> (was mislabelled russian).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Notebook 02 was re-executed top-to-bottom on 2026-07-16</strong> (all 21 code cells, 174 s,\nno errors; the notebook's stored outputs are from that run). <code>data/final</code> now reflects\nthe fixed code — measured results in §E. A copy of the pre-fix <code>data/final</code> was kept\nfor the comparison in §E.</p>\n<p><strong>Still to do:</strong> re-run <code>03_visualisation</code> — its exit-code figure/table change\nmaterially (see §E), and any LaTeX table quoting <code>exit_N</code> is currently wrong.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"d-second-pass-findings-found-while-acting-on-the-authors-questions-all-fixed-re-run\">D. Second-pass findings (found while acting on the author's questions; all fixed + re-run)</h3>\n<p>The author's \"could we infer Praha from names?\" question sent me back into the geography,\nand the trail from the <em>68 % spatial-court agreement</em> — which §E originally, and <strong>wrongly</strong>,\ndismissed as \"structural\" — uncovered the largest geography bugs in the build.</p>\n<h4 id=\"d1-the-lexikon-parser-silently-dropped-982-register-entries-lexikonpy\">D1. The lexikon parser silently dropped 982 register entries — <code>lexikon.py</code></h4>\n<p><code>_UP</code>, the ALL-CAPS headword alphabet, was <code>\"A-ZÁČĎÉĚÍŇÓŔŘŠŤÚŮÝŽ\"</code> — <strong>no German umlauts</strong>.\nEvery Sudeten-exonym headword (<code>ADLERHÜTTE</code>, <code>ADOLFSGRÜN</code>, <code>ALTBÜRGERSDORF</code> — 531 of them)\ntherefore failed to start an entry, and <code>_entries()</code> appended its text to the <strong>previous</strong>\nsettlement, corrupting that settlement's <code>okres_all</code> timeline. A further 451 entries use the\nseparator <code>od r.</code> (\"from year X onwards\") which <code>_HEAD</code> did not know. Symptom that exposed it:\n<code>ARCHLEBOV</code>'s timeline read <code>\"hodonin arletzgrun viz arnoldov (karlovy vary)\"</code>.</p>\n<p>Fixed (<code>_UP</code> += <code>ÄÖÜ</code>; <code>_HEAD</code> accepts <code>v r.|od r.|viz</code>; open-ended <code>od r. Y</code> → <code>y1=9999</code>).\n<strong>Measured:</strong> settlements 18,151 → <strong>18,664</strong> (+513), aliases 9,559 → <strong>10,028</strong>, settlements\nwith a resolved 1910 okres 15,607 → <strong>16,364</strong> (+757), corrupted timelines <strong>390 → 1</strong>.\nNote the corrupted timelines also made the <code>settlement</code> tier's temporal gate <em>over-permissive</em>.</p>\n<h4 id=\"d2-a-third-of-settlement-coordinates-pointed-at-the-wrong-village-wikidatapy\">D2. A third of settlement coordinates pointed at the wrong village — <code>wikidata.py</code></h4>\n<p><code>add_settlement_coords</code> carried the same unguarded unique-name fallback as A1: <em>\"only one\n<a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> point has this name → take it\"</em>, with <strong>no okres check</strong>. <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> lists ~6.2k\n<strong>present-day municipalities</strong>; the lexikon holds ~18k <strong>historical</strong> settlements (incl. <em>osady</em>\nand absorbed hamlets), and 2,250 lexikon names are shared by several settlements. So wherever\nfive like-named settlements had one surviving modern obec, <strong>all five inherited its\ncoordinates</strong> — a median <strong>75 km</strong> (p90 223 km, max 387 km) from their own okres.</p>\n<p>Decisive measurement: coordinates that pass okres corroboration land in their own name-based\nokres <strong>87.4 %</strong> of the time; those taken by the fallback, <strong>30.8 %</strong>. Fixed by requiring\nokres corroboration, keeping a fallback only for names unique on <strong>both</strong> sides.\n<strong>Measured:</strong> geocoded settlements 7,248 → <strong>5,907</strong>; coordinate accuracy <strong>68.3 % → 88.1 %</strong>\n(and 88.1 % is a floor — a settlement whose okres changed since 1910 counts as a \"miss\").\nWrong coordinates would have silently corrupted the planned Kelly-2019 spatial SEs; missing\nones merely reduce n, hence correctness over coverage.</p>\n<p><em>Vindicated in passing:</em> <code>spatial.py</code>'s hand-rolled LAEA is <strong>correct</strong> — <code>.prj</code> confirms\nEPSG:3035 with the exact parameters used, <code>forward(10,52)</code> hits the false origin exactly, and\na ±3 km offset grid peaks at <strong>(0,0)</strong>. The 68 % was never a projection or polygon problem.</p>\n<h4 id=\"d3-bare-praha-okres-praha-authors-decision-evidence-backed\">D3. Bare-\"Praha\" → okres Praha (author's decision, evidence-backed)</h4>\n<p>Implemented as <code>records_geo.CROSS_FRAME_WHITELIST</code> — a curated, documented exception to the\ncross-frame guard, assigned at the weakest <code>muni</code> tier so it stays filterable. <strong>All 1,915</strong>\n<a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> Praha-born now resolve: 1,909 → okres Praha (301072), <strong>6</strong> genuinely Slovak (okres\n\"Lučenec\") → járás, <strong>0</strong> unresolved.</p>\n<p><em>Tested first, since names were the obvious alternative:</em> a char-n-gram classifier separates\ncapital-born from Lučenec-born at <strong>AUC 0.955</strong> — but against ~1:2,250 prior odds it flips\n<strong>0 of 1,780</strong> records (max posterior 0.22), while <strong>4.9 %</strong> of <em>confirmed</em> Prague-born score\nLR&gt;10, i.e. name-based assignment would systematically expel the capital's Slovak/Magyar-named\nmigrants — differential misclassification correlated with ethnicity, which is both a covariate\nand plausibly correlated with the outcome. Base rates, not names. Expected cost ≈3/1,780.</p>\n<h4 id=\"d4-cross-frame-attribute-leak-records_geoadd_settlement_attrs\">D4. Cross-frame attribute leak — <code>records_geo.add_settlement_attrs</code></h4>\n<p>Fifth instance of the same family. Its bare-name fallback required lexikon-uniqueness (so it\nwas milder than D2) but had <strong>no cross-frame guard</strong> and ran <em>before</em> the Slovak pass — so a\nSlovak-born record with an unresolved <code>land</code> and a lexikon-unique village name silently took\nCzech <code>municipality_cs</code> / <code>court_id</code> / <code>lat</code> / <code>lon</code> / <code>qid</code>, then received a járás afterwards.\nThe §4 frame-exclusivity assertion did <strong>not</strong> catch it (it only checks <code>okres_id</code> vs\n<code>jaras_id</code>, not attributes). <strong>Measured: 124 records.</strong> Adding <code>exclude_names</code> cut it to 21;\nthe residue were records whose Slovak district string simply isn't in the Majo crosswalk, so\nthe name guard is blind to them. Closed by making the fallback consistent with the <code>muni</code>\ntier it mirrors — <strong>an unresolved raw okres string is positive evidence the place is not\nCzech</strong>, so the bare-name path now requires no okres string at all. <strong>Result: 124 → 21 → 0.</strong></p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"a-bugs-that-affect-the-data\">A. Bugs that affect the data</h3>\n<h4 id=\"a1-slovak-settlement-pass-double-places-czech-born-records-records_geo_huadd_settlement_jaras-largest-finding\">A1. Slovak settlement pass double-places Czech-born records — <code>records_geo_hu.add_settlement_jaras</code> ⚠ largest finding</h4>\n<p><strong>What:</strong> the village pass gates only on <code>{prefix}_jaras_id</code> being empty and the\nmunicipality being present (<code>records_geo_hu.py:190</code>):</p>\n<div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"n\">gate</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">jid_col</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isna</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"o\">&amp;</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">muni_col</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">notna</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n</pre></div>\n\n<p>It never checks <code>{prefix}_okres_id</code>. Since <code>add_jaras_id</code> (correctly) skips\nCzech-placed rows, those rows reach the village pass with an empty <code>jaras_id</code> — and\nany record whose municipality name <strong>uniquely matches a Slovak obec</strong> gets a Slovak\njárás <strong>on top of</strong> its correct Czech okres.</p>\n<p><strong>Verified impact (final data on disk):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>frame</th>\n<th>role</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">rows with okres_id <strong>and</strong> jaras_id (all tier <code>settlement</code>)</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>legionnaires</td>\n<td>birth</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>6,320</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>legionnaires</td>\n<td>home</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">5,576</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>habsburg</td>\n<td>birth</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>4,287</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>habsburg</td>\n<td>home</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,290</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Examples: <em>Praha (okres Praha)</em> → járás <strong>Halič</strong> (there is a Slovak village\nPraha in okres Lučenec); <em>Poruba (Bílovec)</em> → <strong>Prievidza</strong>; <em>Březolupy\n(Uherské Hradiště)</em> → <strong>Bánovce nad Bebravou</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Downstream contamination:</strong> <code>data/final/analysis/*_census_hu_1910.parquet</code> joins on\n<code>birth_jaras_id</code>, so ~6.3k Czech-born legionnaires currently carry Upper-Hungary\ncensus covariates; any rule of the form \"Slovak-born ⇔ <code>birth_jaras_id</code> notna\" is\nwrong for these rows. (<code>birth_land</code> is unaffected — it says \"Czech lands\", which is\nhow the contradiction shows.)</p>\n<p><strong>Fix (one line + re-run notebook 02 §4/§4b):</strong></p>\n<div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"n\">gate</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">jid_col</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isna</span><span class=\"p\">()</span> <span class=\"o\">&amp;</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"n\">muni_col</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">notna</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>\n<span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">prefix</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">_okres_id\"</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">columns</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">gate</span> <span class=\"o\">&amp;=</span> <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"sa\">f</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"si\">{</span><span class=\"n\">prefix</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"s2\">_okres_id\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">isna</span><span class=\"p\">()</span>          <span class=\"c1\"># only rows the Czech frame left unplaced</span>\n</pre></div>\n\n<p>Optionally also veto positively-foreign lands (Austria/Galicia/Russia…), mirroring\n<code>records_geo.add_birth_settlement_okres</code>'s <code>muni</code>-tier veto — the pass currently has\nno land gate at all, so e.g. a Galician village uniquely matching a Slovak obec name\nwould still be placed in Slovakia.</p>\n<p><em>Note: <code>PLACE_MATCHING.md</code> correctly states the intent (\"runs only on records the\nCzech frame left unplaced\") — the code violates the doc. <code>utils/validation/\nbuild_validation_workbook.py</code> re-runs the same passes in-process and inherits the bug.</em></p>\n<h4 id=\"a2-illegitimacy_rate-is-silently-never-created-census_labelsadd_shares_cis\">A2. <code>illegitimacy_rate</code> is silently never created — <code>census_labels.add_shares_cis</code></h4>\n<p>The gate checks for <code>births_livebirths_illegitimate_total</code> and\n<code>births_livebirths_total_total</code> (<code>census_labels.py:395</code>),\nbut the rename actually produces <code>births_livebirths_illegitimate</code> and\n<code>births_livebirths_total</code> — the trailing <code>_z</code> (Zusammen) token is dropped by\n<code>_compose</code>'s single-letter filter (<code>[a-z]</code> matches <code>z</code>, and <code>z</code> is not in the\n<code>(\"a\",\"b\",\"c\",\"d\",\"m\",\"w\")</code> keep-tuple). Verified: the final\n<code>census_okres_1910</code> panel has <code>literacy_rate</code> and <code>pop_total</code> but <strong>no\n<code>illegitimacy_rate</code></strong>, while <code>census_codebook.{csv,md}</code> claims it exists.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> use the real names in <code>add_shares_cis</code>:</p>\n<div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"p\">{</span><span class=\"s2\">\"births_livebirths_illegitimate\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"s2\">\"births_livebirths_total\"</span><span class=\"p\">}</span> <span class=\"o\">&lt;=</span> <span class=\"nb\">set</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"o\">.</span><span class=\"n\">columns</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>\n    <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"illegitimacy_rate\"</span><span class=\"p\">]</span> <span class=\"o\">=</span> <span class=\"n\">_safe_div</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"births_livebirths_illegitimate\"</span><span class=\"p\">],</span>\n                                         <span class=\"n\">out</span><span class=\"p\">[</span><span class=\"s2\">\"births_livebirths_total\"</span><span class=\"p\">])</span>\n</pre></div>\n\n<p>(Do <strong>not</strong> instead add <code>\"z\"</code> to the keep-tuple — that would rename ~100 existing\ncolumns and break the codebook crosswalk.) Re-run notebook 02 §3b.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a3-exit-code-tokenizer-fabricates-codes-exit_codesparse_exit_codes-upgraded\">A3. Exit-code tokenizer fabricates codes — <code>exit_codes.parse_exit_codes</code> ⚠ upgraded</h4>\n<p>Three related defects (the third — by far the largest — surfaced only during the\nfix-verification pass, because it needs the notebook's <code>normalize_missing</code> step to\ntrigger):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><code>_DROP_TOKENS</code> is a <strong>no-op</strong>: <code>found -= _DROP_TOKENS</code> runs after tokenisation,\n   but the tokenizer only ever adds canonical codes, and no drop token is\n   canonical, so nothing is ever removed.</li>\n<li>The greedy letter-scan walks <em>through</em> unknown tokens, so an unrecognised token\n   containing a code letter emits that code: <code>\"MD\"</code> → <strong>D</strong>, <code>\"DMD\"</code> → DM + D,\n   <code>\"Prop.\"</code> → <strong>P</strong> (59 rows!), <code>\"Super\"</code> → <strong>S</strong> (16 rows).</li>\n<li><strong>The NaN guard only caught <code>float</code> NaN</strong> — but <code>clean.normalize_missing</code> turns\n   missing/<code>?</code> cells into <code>pd.NA</code>, which sailed through as the string <code>\"&lt;NA&gt;\"</code>,\n   whose letter <strong>N was scanned into a fabricated \"Missing\" exit code on every\n   empty cell: 26,386 rows</strong> (26,336 missing + 50 <code>?</code>). The final data's\n   <code>exit_N = 27,034</code> is ~97% fabricated; the true count is <strong>644</strong>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Fixed (v1.1.0), verified against the old build — every delta named:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>code</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">old build</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">post-fix</th>\n<th style=\"text-align: right;\">Δ</th>\n<th>cause</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>N</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">27,034</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">644</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−26,389</td>\n<td><code>\"&lt;NA&gt;\"</code>/<code>\"?\"</code> fabrication (item 3) + one <code>N?</code> recovered</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>P</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">152</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">93</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−59</td>\n<td>\"Prop.\" fabrication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>S</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,096</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,080</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−16</td>\n<td>\"Super\" (+ DMCSA) fabrication</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D</strong></td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3,719</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">3,717</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\"><strong>−2</strong></td>\n<td><code>MD</code>/<code>DMD</code> refused by decision (legion_id 113629, 18666 — plausibly DM, not certain)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>DM</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">61,506</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">61,505</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−1</td>\n<td><code>DMD</code> refused</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>X</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">17,531</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">17,530</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">−1</td>\n<td>\"Xne\" refused (would also have fabricated N)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>ČSA</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,986</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">2,987</td>\n<td style=\"text-align: right;\">+1</td>\n<td>glued <code>DMCSA</code> now correctly parses as DM + ČSA</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Mechanics of the fix: NA guard covers every missing scalar (<code>not isinstance(raw,\nstr) and pd.isna(raw)</code>); extraction is token-wise — drop-list checked first, <code>?</code>\nuncertainty marks stripped (<code>( D? )</code> keeps its D), glued variants substituted\n(<code>DMM</code>→DM, <code>DMCSA</code>→DM+ČSA), and a token must parse <strong>completely</strong> as codes or is\nrefused whole. <strong>Downstream:</strong> any prior output using <code>exit_N</code> (the 20% \"Missing\"\nshare!) is wrong — treat \"no exit code\" (now 30,782 rows) as censoring, not as a\nMissing outcome. <code>deserted</code> was effectively unaffected (−2).</p>\n<h4 id=\"a4-slovak-county-form-lookup-misses-6-adjectival-forms-records_geo_hu_county_key\">A4. Slovak county-form lookup misses ~6 adjectival forms — <code>records_geo_hu._county_key</code></h4>\n<p><code>_STOLICA</code> stripping only removes literal suffixes (<code>\"ska\"</code>, <code>\"stol.\"</code> …), so\nadjectival county names whose noun does not end at the <code>ska</code> boundary never reduce to\nthe curated <code>megye_sk</code> noun:</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>record form (count in unplaced legion births)</th>\n<th>after <code>_county_key</code></th>\n<th>curated noun</th>\n<th>hit?</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nitranská stol. (20)</td>\n<td><code>nitran</code></td>\n<td>Nitra</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hontianská stol. (22)</td>\n<td><code>hontian</code></td>\n<td>Hont</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Trenčianska / Turčianska / Oravská / Bratislavská (tail)</td>\n<td><code>trencian</code> / <code>turcian</code> / <code>orav</code> / <code>bratislav</code></td>\n<td>Trenčín / Turiec / Orava / Bratislava</td>\n<td>✗</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zvolenská, Liptovská, Spišská, Šarišská, Zemplínska, Gemerská, Abovská, Novohradská, Tekovská</td>\n<td>(noun)</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>✓</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Also: <strong>\"Bereg\"</strong> (20 records) misses because the curation stores <code>sk = \"Berehovo\"</code>\n(the seat town), and the <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-megye-hu\" data-gl=\"megye\">megye</a> lookup has no Magyar-name or alt-name keys. Curation\nrows <code>Ung → \"Už\"</code> and <code>Ugocsa → \"Uhoča\"</code> look suspect (expected <em>Užská/Už</em> region\nform and <em>Ugoča</em>) — verify against the record strings actually observed.</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong> add a small explicit adjectival→noun map for the ~16 counties (or <code>sk_alt</code>\nmegye rows in <code>district_sk_curation.csv</code>, and key <code>megye_lut</code> on Magyar + alt names\ntoo). Total recovery ≈ 100+ records into the <code>megye</code> tier.</p>\n<h4 id=\"a5-point-in-polygon-ignores-holes-spatialcourtlocatorlocate\">A5. Point-in-polygon ignores holes — <code>spatial.CourtLocator.locate</code></h4>\n<div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"nb\">any</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">_point_in_ring</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">px</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">py</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">r</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">rings</span><span class=\"p\">):</span>   <span class=\"c1\"># WRONG for polygons with holes</span>\n</pre></div>\n\n<p>Even-odd containment over a multi-ring ESRI polygon requires <strong>parity</strong>, not <code>any</code>:\na point inside a hole (a city court district cut out of the surrounding rural\ncourt's polygon) still tests \"inside\" the rural polygon's outer ring, so whichever\npolygon comes first in <code>.dbf</code> order wins. This plausibly explains part of the\ndocumented 9% disagreement with the name-based okres (\"city/rural twins\").</p>\n<p><strong>Fix:</strong></p>\n<div class=\"highlight\"><pre><span></span><span class=\"k\">if</span> <span class=\"nb\">sum</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">_point_in_ring</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"n\">px</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">py</span><span class=\"p\">,</span> <span class=\"n\">r</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"k\">for</span> <span class=\"n\">r</span> <span class=\"ow\">in</span> <span class=\"n\">rings</span><span class=\"p\">)</span> <span class=\"o\">%</span> <span class=\"mi\">2</span> <span class=\"o\">==</span> <span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"p\">:</span>\n</pre></div>\n\n<p>Affects <code>court_id</code> on <code>settlements_1910</code> and hence <code>birth_court_*</code> — re-run §3c/§4\nafter fixing and re-check the 91% agreement stat (it should rise).</p>\n<h4 id=\"a6-pop_total-sums-without-min_count-census_labelsadd_shares_cis\">A6. <code>pop_total</code> sums without <code>min_count</code> — <code>census_labels.add_shares_cis</code></h4>\n<p><code>out[inh].sum(axis=1)</code> turns an all-NaN row into <strong>0</strong>, not NA. Currently no okres\nhas missing settlement-size classes (verified), so this is <strong>latent</strong> — but one\nmissing workbook away from silently writing population 0. Fix:\n<code>.sum(axis=1, min_count=1)</code> (or <code>min_count=len(inh)</code> to require all six classes).</p>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"b-logic-robustness-fix-when-touching-the-module\">B. Logic &amp; robustness (fix when touching the module)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>B1 · ranks.py — Habsburg <code>podpraporčík</code> mapping is undocumented.</strong> The Habsburg\n  rules map <code>podpraporcik</code> → <em>praporčík</em> (ord 9, common 9), while the Legion system\n  scores <em>podpraporčík</em> at ord 6 / common 6. The k.u.k. army had no podpraporčík;\n  <code>rank_ladders.md</code> §A documents dropping <code>podporučík</code>/<code>rotmistr</code> but is silent on\n  this token. Either drop it (like podporučík) or document the choice. Check n first.</li>\n<li><strong>B2 · ranks.py — <code>nadlékař</code> (n≈20) documented but unparsed.</strong> §A1b calls it\n  officer-equivalent (90% · P408); the parser leaves <code>&lt;NA&gt;</code>. Align doc and code\n  (either add a rule or state in the doc that medical officers are deliberately unscored).</li>\n<li><strong>B3 · education.py — <code>pok.</code> (pokračovací škola) scored as Upper secondary.</strong>\n  Continuation/apprentice schooling is conventionally <em>below</em> reálka/gymnasium.\n  Because rules are first-match, <code>\"ob. 5 mě. 3 pok. 3\"</code> → Upper secondary. Consider\n  a separate <code>Vocational continuation</code> level or fold into Lower secondary; the module\n  docstring itself flags the mapping as unverified. Also: dotted-token rules miss\n  full words and dot-less variants (<code>gymnasium</code>, <code>měšťanka</code>, <code>2mě</code>, <code>3mě</code>, bare\n  <code>pokr.</code>, <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-hosp-cs\" data-gl=\"hosp.\"><code>hosp.</code></a>, <a class=\"gl\" href=\"#gl-odb-cs\" data-gl=\"odb.\"><code>odb.</code></a>, <code>kadetka</code>) — coverage is 96.7%, unmatched ≈3.5k with\n  systematic classes recoverable by ~8 extra tokens.\n  <strong>→ Fixed 2026-09-02</strong> (<code>education.py</code> 2.0.0, <a class=\"dref\" href=\"#decisions-d-08\">D-08</a>). Not by extra tokens on the same\n  design: the string is an <em>itinerary</em>, so it is tokenised and the highest institution\n  taken. <code>pok.</code> became a boolean rather than a level, because <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"lassnigg_2015\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Lassnigg (2015)</a> shows the\n  Fortbildungsschule was legally part of <strong>compulsory</strong> schooling and required no\n  credential to enter — so folding it into Lower secondary, as this item proposed,\n  would have been the same error one rung down.</li>\n<li><strong>B4 · affiliations.py — <code>\"demokrat\"</code> → <code>pokr</code> double-count is real but tiny.</strong>\n  Verified: 13 rows match both <code>soc_dem</code> and <code>pokr</code> (e.g. \"sociální demokrat\").\n  Fix by skipping the bare <code>demokrat</code> token when a more specific party already\n  matched. (CLAUDE.md lists this as a known issue — still open in v1.2.0.)\n  Also <code>\"rus\"</code> ⊂ <em>Rusín</em> would mislabel a Rusyn as Russian — check the raw values.</li>\n<li><strong>B5 · theater.py — fragile substrings.</strong> <code>\"RA\"</code> (uppercase) → Russia and\n  <code>\"Battalion\"</code> → Britain/Dominions are one odd string away from misfiring;\n  case-sensitivity is what keeps \"FRANCIE\"-type strings safe today. Fine at the\n  achieved 96.5% coverage, but document that pass-2 majority vote propagates any\n  pass-1 error regiment-wide.</li>\n<li><strong>B6 · records_geo.py — dead code + silent tie-breaks.</strong> <code>_FOREIGN_LANDS</code>\n  (line 173) is never used (the muni-tier veto uses <code>!= \"Czech lands\"</code>); delete or\n  use it. <code>build_okres_lookup</code> keeps the first of two same-named court districts in\n  different okresy silently — print/log duplicates once so the ambiguity is auditable.\n  <code>add_birth_settlement_okres</code> and <code>add_settlement_jaras</code> both <code>reset_index(drop=True)</code>\n  — harmless in the notebook (RangeIndex) but a footgun for any caller with a\n  meaningful index.</li>\n<li><strong>B7 · records_geo.py — <code>add_settlement_attrs</code> unique-name test via <code>id(rd)</code>.</strong>\n  Settlements without a <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"wikidata\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">Wikidata</a> QID use the Python object id as a stand-in key;\n  CPython id-reuse makes the uniqueness test opportunistic. Outcome-safe today\n  (a qid-less \"unique\" name can never resolve to a row), but replace with an explicit\n  per-row counter for clarity.</li>\n<li><strong>B8 · records_geo.py — okres-name normalisation gaps.</strong> \"Ústí n. Labem\"\n  (50 records) fails because <code>_norm</code> does not expand <code>n.</code>/<code>n</code> → <code>nad</code> (token-set\n  ratio lands just under the 90 threshold). \"Nové Benátky\" (46) fails because the\n  curation stores the <strong>modern</strong> name (<em>Benátky nad Jizerou</em>) in the 1910 <code>cs</code> slot —\n  against the stated <code>cs</code> = 1910-name convention. Add an <code>n.</code> → <code>nad</code> expansion in\n  <code>_norm</code> (or aliases), and fix the curation row (put <em>Nové Benátky</em> in <code>cs</code>,\n  <em>Benátky nad Jizerou</em> in <code>cs_modern</code>). \"Česká Třebová\" (21) names a town that was\n  not a 1910 court/okres — candidate for <code>ALIAS_OVERRIDE</code> → okres Lanškroun after a\n  quick historical check.</li>\n<li><strong>B9 · linkage.py — recall limitations worth stating in the paper.</strong> Surname-initial\n  blocking cannot match V/W-type first-letter spelling variants (Veselý/Wesely);\n  both criteria require exact birth year, so off-by-one archival years are dropped\n  by design. Fine (precision-first), but say so in the linkage appendix.\n  <code>_LEGION_RE</code> could in principle hit non-Czechoslovak \"legie\" mentions (Polish\n  legions) — the okres+year+name conjunction makes a false confirmed match unlikely.</li>\n<li><strong>B10 · census.py — silent duplicate-column drop.</strong> When two census files map to\n  the same final column name, the later file's column is dropped without a message\n  (<code>dup</code> check in <code>build_census_panel</code>). Add a print.</li>\n<li><strong>B11 · census_labels.py — ~20 duplicate dict keys in <code>DE_TOKENS</code>/<code>HU_TOKENS</code></strong>\n  (e.g. <code>vergleich</code>, <code>endurteil</code>, <code>pensionsfonds</code>, <code>nepes10</code>). All duplicates map to\n  identical values, so behaviour is unaffected — deduplicate for hygiene.</li>\n<li><strong>B12 · wikidata.add_birth_coords is unused</strong> (the notebook gets coordinates via\n  <code>records_geo.add_settlement_attrs</code>); if it is ever revived, note it lacks the\n  foreign-land veto that <code>add_settlement_attrs</code> has.</li>\n<li><strong>B13 · units.py — <code>pol.děl.pl.</code> labels as generic \"Artillery regiment\"</strong> because\n  <code>děl.pl.</code> precedes <code>pol.děl.</code> in <code>UNIT_TYPE_MAP</code>. Reorder if the field-artillery\n  distinction ever matters (plot-level only).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"c-documentation-drift-code-is-right-docs-lag-or-vice-versa\">C. Documentation drift (code is right, docs lag — or vice versa)</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>C1 · README.md is two reorganisations stale.</strong> Points to <code>02_eda.ipynb</code>,\n  lists 5 of ~20 parsing modules, pre-reorg tree (<code>reference/</code> described as\n  \"reading list\", no <code>library/</code>, no <code>data/final</code> split, no geo/census stage,\n  no 03 notebook). Rewrite against the current CLAUDE.md tree.</li>\n<li><strong>C2 · linkage.py docstrings.</strong> \"~90 pairs satisfy both\" → actual <strong>144</strong>;\n  \"~2,200 individuals\" → <strong>2,239</strong> (1,776 high / 463 medium; verified from\n  <code>overlap_matches.csv</code> — CLAUDE.md's numbers are the correct ones). Also the\n  gold-standard letters are swapped between the module header (A = DOB) and\n  <code>_tier</code>'s docstring (calls DOB \"gold standard B\").</li>\n<li><strong>C3 · rank_ladders.md.</strong> Status section still says the common scale is \"still to\n  be built\" and cites <code>ranks.py</code> v1.2.0 — it is built (v1.3.0: <code>_common</code>, <code>_band</code>,\n  <code>add_rank_reset</code>; §G3 already uses it). §G's worked example \"četař → vojín = −2\"\n  disagrees with the implemented common scale (−3); §G3 acknowledges the schematic\n  looseness but §G itself should be corrected. §B header: <code>štábní rotmistr</code> is\n  \"6⁺\" in the doc, ties at 6 in code (documented as intentional in the module —\n  add one line in §B).</li>\n<li><strong>C4 · census_codebook.md / census_codebook.csv</strong> list <code>illegitimacy_rate</code> (see A2)\n  — true only after the A2 fix and §3b re-run.</li>\n<li><strong>C5 · notebook 02 cell 27 (data-dictionary rows)</strong> documents census columns under\n  their <strong>German</strong> names (<code>umgspr_*</code>, <code>rel_romisch_katholisch</code>, <code>bild_*</code>…) although\n  the panel has been English since census_labels v1.1 (<code>lang_*</code>, <code>rel_*</code>, <code>lit_*</code>).\n  Rewrite those rows with the English family names (and point at\n  <code>reference/census/census_codebook.csv</code> as the authoritative list).</li>\n<li><strong>C6 · ranks.add_rank_columns docstring</strong> says \"seven rank columns\"; it appends\n  <strong>nine</strong> (<code>_clean, _en, _ord, _ord_low, _jd, _branch, _tier, _common, _band</code>).</li>\n<li><strong>C7 · dates.py docstring</strong> says \"Defaults to all three configured columns\" —\n  there are four; the Added-columns list omits <code>death_date</code>/<code>death_year_only</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>C8 · clean.strip_place_markers</strong> docstring says \"trailing <code>+</code>\" but the code\n  replaces <strong>all</strong> <code>+</code> occurrences (fine in practice — document it).</li>\n<li><strong>C9 · PLACE_MATCHING.md</strong> — correct on intent; after the A1 fix its \"runs only on\n  unplaced births\" claim becomes true again. Re-verify the coverage table after\n  A1/A4/A5 fixes.</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"checked-and-found-sound\">Checked and found sound</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Rank ladders vs. code:</strong> both ordinal maps match <code>rank_ladders.md</code> §A/§B row by\n  row (incl. kadet-aspirant 6 &lt; kadet 7 &lt; důst. zástupce 8 &lt; praporčík 9; Legion\n  podporučík 8 &lt; poručík 9 &lt; nadporučík 10; štábní kapitán collapsed to 11,\n  podkapitán to 10; kornet 9; pracovník/občan at 1; non-ranks dropped).</li>\n<li><strong>Rank rule ordering:</strong> every substring-containment trap checked (podplukovník\n  before plukovník, nadporučík/podporučík before poručík, podpraporčík before\n  praporčík, štábní/pod- captains before kapitán, generálmajor before major,\n  oberstleutnant before oberst, feldmarschalleutnant before feldmarschall,\n  kadet-aspirant before kadet); drop tokens are word-bounded so <code>zak</code> cannot strike\n  <code>zakopnik</code>. First-match-wins resolves correctly in all checked cases.</li>\n<li><strong>Common scale / reset:</strong> <code>RANK_COMMON</code> is name-anchored exactly as §G specifies;\n  <code>rank_reset</code> propagates <code>&lt;NA&gt;</code> when either side is unresolved; the band index is\n  monotone (OR-1…9 → 1…9, OF-1…10 → 10…19). §G3's reliability decomposition\n  (82% of demotions on the 100%-confidence spine; 99.8% sign agreement between fine\n  and band reset) matches the implemented machinery.</li>\n<li><strong>Census joins:</strong> exact-code joins verified end-to-end (<code>IDCourtdistr = court_id</code>;\n  <code>court_id // 1000 = okres_id</code>; <code>kod = unit_id</code>); court-level aggregation sums\n  counts only (all court-level workbooks are counts); merged-header expansion via\n  openpyxl is the right call for the multi-row German headers.</li>\n<li><strong>Linkage:</strong> precision-first tiering implemented as documented; <code>add_overlap_flags</code>\n  deduplicates per side keeping the highest tier; blocking on (year, initial) is\n  consistent with both criteria requiring exact year. Numbers in CLAUDE.md verified\n  against <code>overlap_matches.csv</code>.</li>\n<li><strong>Date parsing:</strong> century fix (<code>&gt;1930</code> → −100 if in range), double-date rescue,\n  year-only fallback with precision flags — all correct; clip ranges sensible.</li>\n<li><strong>Land classification:</strong> the positive-classification design (<code>okres_id</code> ⇒ Czech\n  lands; curated Slovak district list; text markers for the rest) supersedes the\n  weaker text-only <code>locations.py</code> land, as documented.</li>\n<li><strong><code>sum_ok</code> in lexikon_sk:</strong> treating unprinted mother-tongue columns as true\n  zeros (<code>fillna(0)</code> in shares) is correct given the source prints nothing for zero.</li>\n<li><strong>requirements.txt</strong> covers every import (rapidfuzz, pypdf, pdfplumber, openpyxl,\n  pyarrow, requests, bs4/lxml).</li>\n</ul>\n<hr>\n<h3 id=\"e-post-fix-verification-measured-on-the-re-run-datafinal-2026-07-16\">E. Post-fix verification — <strong>measured on the re-run <code>data/final</code></strong> (2026-07-16)</h3>\n<p>Notebook 02 re-executed in full (21/21 cells, 174 s, exit 0); every figure below is read\noff the freshly written files and compared against a copy of the pre-fix build.</p>\n<div class=\"tblwrap\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>check</th>\n<th>pre-fix</th>\n<th><strong>post-fix</strong></th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>rows in BOTH frames</strong> (okres ∧ járás) — <em>birth</em></td>\n<td>6,320 legion / 4,287 habsburg</td>\n<td><strong>0 / 0</strong> ✅ (asserted in §4)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rows in BOTH frames — <em>home</em></td>\n<td>5,576 / 2,290</td>\n<td><strong>0 / 0</strong> ✅</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>row counts (unchanged, no join blowup)</td>\n<td>132,993 / 186,192</td>\n<td><strong>132,993 / 186,192</strong> ✅</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>birth_okres_id</code></td>\n<td>101,297 / 120,230</td>\n<td><strong>101,439</strong> (+142) / <strong>120,116</strong> (−114)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>birth_jaras_id</code></td>\n<td>18,188 / 28,554</td>\n<td><strong>11,473</strong> (−6,715) / <strong>21,539</strong> (−7,015)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Czech-born rows carrying <strong>HU census</strong> in <code>*_census_hu_1910</code></td>\n<td>6,320 / 4,287</td>\n<td><strong>0 / 0</strong> ✅</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SK <code>megye</code> tier</td>\n<td>~0</td>\n<td>630 legion / 43 habsburg (adjectival county forms now resolve)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SK <code>settlement</code> tier</td>\n<td>6,320 / 4,287 (mostly bogus)</td>\n<td>284 / 1,020 — corroborated or positively-Slovak</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>SK county resolved, of Slovak-land births</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td><strong>99.7 %</strong> legion · <strong>99.3 %</strong> habsburg</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>bare \"Praha\" births</td>\n<td><strong>all 2,693 legion + 1,909 habsburg → járás Halič</strong></td>\n<td>legion 2,680 → okres Praha (301072), <strong>0</strong> in any járás; habsburg 129 okres-resolved + <strong>6</strong> genuinely Slovak (okres \"Lučenec\"), rest NA (open decision below)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>illegitimacy_rate</code></td>\n<td>absent</td>\n<td>present, <strong>158/158</strong> okresy, mean <strong>0.108</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>pop_total</code></td>\n<td>(latent 0-risk)</td>\n<td>no zero rows; min_count guard</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>exit_N</code></td>\n<td>27,034</td>\n<td><strong>645</strong> — 26,389 fabricated values removed</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><code>deserted</code></td>\n<td>3,719</td>\n<td><strong>3,717</strong> (−2: the refused MD/DMD)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>rows with no exit code (⇒ censored)</td>\n<td>4,321</td>\n<td><strong>30,770</strong></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>linkage</td>\n<td>2,239 = 1,776 + 463</td>\n<td><strong>unchanged</strong> (2,239 = 1,776 + 463; 144 both) ✅</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>settlements crosswalk</td>\n<td>18,150</td>\n<td><strong>18,151</strong> (curated Praha row)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>spatial parity fix</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>changes <strong>0</strong> live assignments (only 7/339 polygons multi-ring); correctness hardening</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>point-in-polygon sanity</td>\n<td>—</td>\n<td>Praha centre→Vinohrady, Brno→Brno, Plzeň→Plzeň, Olomouc→Olomouc (město), Cheb, Trutnov — all historically exact</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>Downstream consequence — <code>03_visualisation</code> must be re-run:</strong> the exit-code\ndistribution changes materially (the \"Missing\" bar collapses from 20.3 % to 0.5 %, and\n\"no code parsed\" rises to 23.1 %). Any existing LaTeX table or figure quoting <code>exit_N</code>\nis wrong.</p>\n<h4 id=\"corrected-doc-claim-then-corrected-again-see-d\">Corrected doc claim → then corrected again (see §D)</h4>\n<p><code>spatial.py</code> claimed <strong>91 %</strong> agreement between the spatial court's parent okres and the\nname-based <code>okres_id</code>; the measured figure was <strong>68.3 %</strong>. This section first attributed that\nto 1910 city structure — <strong>that was wrong</strong>, and chasing it properly (§D2) showed it was\nmostly a <em>coordinate</em> bug: a third of settlement geocodes pointed at a like-named village a\nmedian 75 km away. After the D-fixes the figure is <strong>88.1 %</strong>, and the residual is genuinely\nstructural (okres boundaries changed since 1910; city/rural twins; modern centroids).\n<code>court_id</code> remains a complementary juridical layer — do <strong>not</strong> use spatial parentage to\nvalidate or replace the name-based <code>okres_id</code>. Lesson recorded: a suspicious aggregate\ndeserves a root-cause test, not a plausible story.</p>\n<h4 id=\"resolved-bare-praha-births-okres-praha-authors-call-2026-07-16\">Resolved — bare-\"Praha\" births → okres Praha (author's call, 2026-07-16)</h4>\n<p>Whitelisted; see §D3 for the mechanism and the evidence (incl. the name-classifier test that\nargued <em>against</em> using names). All 1,915 <a class=\"cite\" data-ref=\"vua_db\" href=\"#references\" onclick=\"return bibGo(event)\">VÚA</a> Praha-born now resolve; 6 genuinely Slovak ones\nkeep their járás; expected cost ≈3 misplacements in 1,780.</p>\n<p>Done post-run: <code>PLACE_MATCHING.md</code> coverage table refreshed with the measured numbers;\n<code>PIPELINE.md</code> / <code>VARIABLES.md</code> aligned. <strong>Outstanding: re-run <code>03_visualisation</code>.</strong></p><nav class=\"seealso\"><span class=\"sal\">See also</span><a href=\"#decisions\" onclick=\"show('decisions')\"><b>Decisions</b><span>Register of every judgement call, with a link to the argument.</span></a><a href=\"#validation-register\" onclick=\"show('validation-register')\"><b>Validation register</b><span>Every check, and what each one is blind to.</span></a><a href=\"#qa\" onclick=\"show('qa')\"><b>Data QA</b><span>Invariant checks on the current build.</span></a></nav></div>");
